<% content_for :title, "Evaluation - Recourse" %>
<h1>Classifier evaluation</h1>
<p class="lede">Forty labelled borrower replies, run through the deterministic baseline and
through the model. The headline accuracy is the least interesting number here. What the
set is for is finding the misses that cost a lender money or a complaint, and there is one.</p>

<% rules = @run["rules"]; model = @run["model"] %>

<div class="grid">
  <div class="stat"><b><%= rules ? "#{rules['accuracy']}%" : "—" %></b><span>rules baseline (<%= rules&.dig("hits") %>/<%= rules&.dig("total") %>)</span></div>
  <div class="stat good"><b><%= model ? "#{model['accuracy']}%" : "—" %></b><span><%= model&.dig("classifier") || "model" %> (<%= model&.dig("hits") %>/<%= model&.dig("total") %>)</span></div>
  <div class="stat <%= (model&.dig("costly")).to_i.positive? ? "warn" : "good" %>"><b><%= model&.dig("costly") || 0 %></b><span>costly misses</span></div>
  <div class="stat"><b><%= model ? "#{(model['seconds'].to_f / model['total'].to_i).round(1)}s" : "—" %></b><span>per reply, uncached</span></div>
</div>

<% if @run["ran_at"] %>
  <p class="muted" style="font-size:13px">Recorded <%= @run["ran_at"] %>. The numbers on this page are a recorded
  run, not a live one — forty uncached calls take about two minutes, which is not something to do on a page load.
  Use the box below to watch a single reply go through live.</p>
<% end %>

<h2>Try one live</h2>
<div class="panel">
  <%= form_with url: try_evaluation_path, method: :post, local: true do %>
    <label for="body">A borrower reply</label>
    <textarea name="body" id="body" placeholder="e.g. I can do 300 a month, that is genuinely all I have."><%= @body %></textarea>
    <div class="row" style="margin-top:12px">
      <button class="primary" type="submit">Read it</button>
      <span class="muted" style="font-size:12.5px">
        <%= @configured ? "Live model call, structured output, temperature 0." : "No API key present — the rules baseline will answer." %>
      </span>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <% if @result %>
    <div class="readout" style="margin-top:18px">
      <div><dt>intent</dt><dd><%= @result.intent&.tr("_", " ") %></dd></div>
      <div><dt>confidence</dt><dd><%= @result.confidence_pct %>%</dd></div>
      <div><dt>promised</dt><dd><%= @result.promised_on || "—" %></dd></div>
      <div><dt>amount</dt><dd><%= @result.promised_amount_cents ? number_to_currency(@result.promised_amount_cents / 100.0) : "—" %></dd></div>
      <div><dt>read by</dt><dd><%= @result.classifier %></dd></div>
    </div>
    <% if @result.raw&.dig("evidence_span").present? %>
      <p class="muted" style="font-size:13px;margin:10px 0 0">Evidence cited: <em>“<%= @result.raw["evidence_span"] %>”</em></p>
    <% end %>
    <% if @result.error.present? %>
      <p class="flash bad" style="margin-top:12px">Fell back to the baseline: <code><%= @result.error %></code></p>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</div>

<% if model && model["misses"].present? %>
  <h2>Where it is wrong</h2>
  <p>Not all misses are equal. Filing a borrower's question as <em>unclear</em> costs a servicer
  thirty seconds. Reading a <strong>hardship</strong> as a <strong>promise to pay</strong> schedules a
  draft against an account the borrower has just told you is empty, which returns, which charges
  them another NSF fee, which is the thing this product exists to reduce. The eval marks that
  second class separately.</p>

  <% model["misses"].each do |m| %>
    <div class="panel <%= "tight" unless m["costly"] %>">
      <p style="margin:0 0 8px">
        <% if m["costly"] %><span class="pill review">costly</span> <% end %>
        <span class="num"><%= m["id"] %></span>
        <span class="muted">— expected <strong><%= m["expected"].tr("_", " ") %></strong>,
        read as <strong><%= m["got"].tr("_", " ") %></strong> at <%= m["confidence"] %>% confidence</span>
      </p>
      <blockquote style="margin:0 0 8px;padding-left:14px;border-left:3px solid var(--rule);color:var(--ink-2);white-space:pre-wrap"><%= m["body"] %></blockquote>
      <p class="muted" style="font-size:13px;margin:0"><%= m["note"] %></p>
      <% if m["costly"] %>
        <p style="font-size:13.5px;margin:12px 0 0"><strong>Why this one matters.</strong>
        The borrower is not promising, they are telling you what they can survive. The model is
        confident — 80% — and confidence is exactly what makes it dangerous: a threshold rule
        would let it straight through. This is the case that decides the architecture. A
        classification here never becomes a scheduled promise on its own; a servicer promotes it,
        and the promotion is the event. That boundary is not caution for its own sake, it is the
        response to a failure that this set reproduces every run.</p>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
<% end %>

<h2>Per intent</h2>
<div class="scroller">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Intent</th><th>Baseline</th><th><%= model&.dig("classifier") || "model" %></th><th>n</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <% (model&.dig("per_intent") || []).each do |row| %>
        <% base = (rules&.dig("per_intent") || []).find { |r| r["intent"] == row["intent"] } %>
        <tr>
          <td><%= row["intent"].tr("_", " ") %></td>
          <td class="num muted"><%= base ? "#{base['pct']}%" : "—" %></td>
          <td class="num"><%= row["pct"] %>%</td>
          <td class="num muted"><%= row["n"] %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<% if model&.dig("confusion").present? %>
  <h2>Confusion matrix <span class="muted" style="font-weight:400;font-size:13px">— rows are the label, columns are the reading</span></h2>
  <div class="scroller">
    <table class="matrix">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>expected \ read as</th><% Reply::INTENTS.each do |i| %><th><%= i.tr("_", " ") %></th><% end %></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <% Reply::INTENTS.each do |exp| %>
          <% row = model["confusion"][exp] %>
          <% next if row.blank? %>
          <tr>
            <td><%= exp.tr("_", " ") %></td>
            <% Reply::INTENTS.each do |got| %>
              <% n = row[got].to_i %>
              <td class="<%= exp == got ? 'diag' : (n.positive? ? 'off' : 'zero') %>"><%= n.zero? ? "·" : n %></td>
            <% end %>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
<% end %>

<h2>On the labelled set</h2>
<div class="prose">
  <p>The first thirty cases were written to sit on the boundaries a servicing shop trips over.
  The model scored <strong>30 of 30</strong> on them. That is not a result, it is a signal that the set
  stopped discriminating, so ten adversarial cases were added: a promise that appears only inside
  quoted history, a sarcastic refusal shaped like agreement, a bookkeeper who says "I will forward
  this", a conditional that commits to nothing, and the negotiation above.</p>
  <p>Accuracy fell from 100% to <strong><%= model&.dig("accuracy") %>%</strong>, which is the number
  worth having. An eval that only ever goes up is measuring the wrong thing. The next ten cases
  should be drawn from real returned mail rather than written, because the failures that matter
  are the ones nobody thought to imagine.</p>
</div>
